//? Title: Weight and Spectral Normalisation: Gains, Power Iteration and Lipschitz Margins
//? Subtitle: Weight normalisation's gradients, their orthogonality and the norm growth that tames large learning rates; spectral normalisation by power iteration, its exact convergence formula and its gradient; and certified robustness of spectrally normalised digit classifiers, using datamunge's nn::weight_normalize, nn::weight_norm_dense, nn::spectral_norm_power_iteration, nn::spectral_norm_dense and nn::lipschitz_bound
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Weight normalisation writes each column of a weight matrix as a gain times a unit direction. Spectral normalisation divides the whole matrix by its largest singular value, estimated by power iteration. This report verifies weight normalisation's gradient formulas to \(4\times10^{-15}\), and shows that the gradient with respect to the direction is orthogonal to it. The squared norm of the direction therefore grows by exactly \(\eta^2\lVert\nabla\rVert^2\) per gradient step, to \(10^{-15}\), and learning rates that differ by a factor of 16 end up with effective rates within a factor of about 2.
For power iteration, an exact formula in terms of the singular values predicts every estimate to \(6\times10^{-12}\). The error falls by \((\sigma_2/\sigma_1)^4\) per iteration, and the gradient of the converged estimate is the outer product of the top singular vectors.
On the digits data, spectrally normalised MLPs have a Lipschitz bound of 6.3 to 22 against 65 for a plain network. That certifies 63–68% of the test images against any perturbation of size 0.25, against 2% for the plain network. Training moves the weights faster than one power iteration per step can follow, so the stored estimates lag the true spectral norms by 2–12%.
)

\toc

> The problem

The scale of a layer's weights matters in two ways. During training it interacts with the learning rate: the same gradient step changes a small weight vector's direction more than a large one's. After training it bounds how much the layer can amplify a change in its input. Both are hard to control when the scale is spread over all the entries of a matrix.
Weight normalisation (Salimans and Kingma, 2016) gives each output unit an explicit length, separate from its direction, which changes the geometry of gradient descent. Spectral normalisation (Miyato et al., 2018) divides each weight matrix by its largest singular value. Every layer is then 1-Lipschitz, which stabilises GAN discriminators and makes a network's sensitivity to its input explicit (Tsuzuku, Sato and Sugiyama, 2018).

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/weight_spectral_norm.cpp`. That example checked weight normalisation's column norms and gradients and showed that the direction's gradient is orthogonal to it. It followed power iteration to the SVD value, with a fresh start and with the vector kept between calls, and checked that the normalised layer shrinks random inputs. All of that is kept.
The two reparameterisations lived in the example helper `examples/nn/common/reparam.hpp`. They are now part of the library in `datamunge/nn/weight_reparam.hpp`, together with a Lipschitz bound for stacks of layers. The report adds the exact convergence formula for power iteration, the norm-growth identity of weight normalisation and certified robustness on a dataset.

> The method

>> Weight normalisation

A dense layer \(y=xW+b\) with weight normalisation stores a direction \(v_j\) and a gain \(g_j\) for every column and uses

$$
W_{:,j}=g_j\,\frac{v_j}{\lVert v_j\rVert},\qquad \lVert W_{:,j}\rVert=|g_j| .
$$
\alttext(Column j of W equals g j times v j divided by the norm of v j, so its norm is the absolute value of g j.)

The library starts with \(g_j=\lVert v_j\rVert\), so the layer initially equals a plain one. Writing \(\hat v_j=v_j/\lVert v_j\rVert\) and \(G_j=\partial L/\partial W_{:,j}\), the chain rule gives

$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial g_j}=G_j\cdot\hat v_j,\qquad \frac{\partial L}{\partial v_j}=\frac{g_j}{\lVert v_j\rVert}\big(G_j-(G_j\cdot\hat v_j)\,\hat v_j\big).
$$
\alttext(dL by dg j equals G j dot v hat j. dL by dv j equals g j over the norm of v j, times G j minus G j dot v hat j times v hat j.)

The direction's gradient is the part of \(G_j\) orthogonal to \(v_j\), scaled by \(g_j/\lVert v_j\rVert\). Two consequences follow. First, a gradient step \(v_j\leftarrow v_j-\eta\nabla_{v_j}\) is orthogonal to \(v_j\), so by Pythagoras

$$
\lVert v_j^{(t+1)}\rVert^2=\lVert v_j^{(t)}\rVert^2+\eta^2\lVert\nabla_{v_j}L\rVert^2 ,
$$
\alttext(The squared norm of v j after the step equals the squared norm before plus eta squared times the squared norm of the gradient.)

exactly, and the norm can only grow. Second, to first order the step changes \(W_{:,j}\) by \(-\eta\,(g_j^2/\lVert v_j\rVert^2)\) times the orthogonal part of \(G_j\). The effective learning rate for the direction of \(W\) is therefore \(\eta g_j^2/\lVert v_j\rVert^2\). Large steps inflate \(\lVert v_j\rVert\) and so lower their own effective rate, which Salimans and Kingma describe as a built-in stabiliser.

>> Spectral normalisation and power iteration

The spectral norm \(\sigma_1(W)\) is the largest singular value, the most \(W\) can stretch a vector: \(\lVert xW\rVert\le\sigma_1\lVert x\rVert\). Spectral normalisation uses \(W/\hat\sigma\), with \(\hat\sigma\) from power iteration. From a vector \(u\) in the output space, each iteration sets

$$
v\leftarrow\frac{uW^\top}{\lVert uW^\top\rVert},\qquad u\leftarrow\frac{vW}{\lVert vW\rVert},\qquad \hat\sigma=vWu^\top=\lVert vW\rVert .
$$
\alttext(v becomes u W transpose, normalised; u becomes v W, normalised; and sigma hat equals v W u transpose, which equals the norm of v W.)

Since \(v\) is a unit vector, \(\hat\sigma\le\sigma_1\): the estimate never overshoots. Let \(W=U\,\mathrm{diag}(\sigma)\,V^\top\), and let \(c_i\) be the coefficients of the starting vector on the columns of \(V\). Then after \(k\) iterations \(u\propto\sum_ic_i\sigma_i^{2k}V_i\) and \(v\propto\sum_ic_i\sigma_i^{2k-1}U_i\), so with \(A_m=\sum_ic_i^2\sigma_i^m\)

$$
\hat\sigma_k=\sqrt{A_{4k}/A_{4k-2}},\qquad 1-\frac{\hat\sigma_k}{\sigma_1}\approx\frac12\sum_{i\ge2}\frac{c_i^2}{c_1^2}\Big(\frac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_1}\Big)^{4k-2}\Big(1-\frac{\sigma_i^2}{\sigma_1^2}\Big).
$$
\alttext(Sigma hat k equals the square root of A 4k over A 4k minus 2; the relative error is approximately one half times the sum over i from 2 of c i squared over c 1 squared, times sigma i over sigma 1 to the power 4k minus 2, times 1 minus sigma i squared over sigma 1 squared.)

The error falls by \((\sigma_2/\sigma_1)^4\) per iteration once the second singular value dominates the sum, which is slow when the top of the spectrum is flat. In training, \(u\) is stored between steps and refined by one iteration per step (Miyato et al., 2018). As long as the weights move slowly, the estimate keeps converging.
The library treats \(u\) and \(v\) as constants, so \(\partial\hat\sigma/\partial W=v^\top u\), which at convergence is \(\partial\sigma_1/\partial W=U_1V_1^\top\).

>> Lipschitz bounds and certified margins

A stack of dense layers with 1-Lipschitz activations such as ReLU satisfies \(\lVert f(x)-f(x')\rVert\le\prod_l\sigma_1(W_l)\,\lVert x-x'\rVert\), so \(L=\prod_l\sigma_1(W_l)\) bounds its Lipschitz constant. `nn::lipschitz_bound` computes it with exact SVDs. Each difference of two logits is then \(\sqrt2L\)-Lipschitz.
A classifier whose true logit exceeds every other by a margin \(\mu\) therefore keeps its prediction under every perturbation with \(\lVert\delta\rVert<\mu/(\sqrt2L)\) (Tsuzuku, Sato and Sugiyama, 2018). This certified radius is a guarantee. An attack such as projected gradient descent (PGD; Madry et al., 2018) gives the complementary empirical upper bound on robust accuracy.
For Gaussian inputs \(x\sim N(0,I_d)\), \(\mathbb E\lVert xW\rVert^2=\lVert W\rVert_F^2=\sum_i\sigma_i^2\). The typical gain of a normalised layer is therefore about \(\sqrt{\sum_i\sigma_i^2/d}/\sigma_1\), far below the bound, which only the top singular direction attains.

> Simple examples

>> Weight-normalised gradients and power iteration

The program builds a weight-normalised layer with 5 inputs and 3 outputs and sets the gains to (0.5, 1.5, 3). It compares the gradients of \(L=\sum y\odot r\) from automatic differentiation with the closed forms. It then estimates the spectral norm of a \(64\times32\) Glorot-uniform matrix by power iteration from a random start, and again with one iteration per call and the vector stored between calls. Finally, it measures how much the normalised layer stretches 1000 random inputs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/spectral_basics.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

double fmax_abs(const Tensor& t) { return nn::max(nn::abs(t)).item(); }

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // Weight normalisation of a dense layer with 5 inputs and 3 outputs, batch 4, loss L = sum(y ⊙ r) so dL/dy = r.
  nn::Generator g(1);
  const Tensor x = nn::normal({4, 5}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64), r = nn::normal({4, 3}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
  nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
  auto wn = nn::weight_norm_dense(p, "d", x, 3);
  std::print("At creation: max |W − v| = {:.1e} (g starts at the column norms of v).\n", fmax_abs(wn.W - p.at("d/v")));
  {
    nn::NoGradGuard ng;
    p.set("d/g", nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{0.5, 1.5, 3.0}, {3}));
  }
  wn = nn::weight_norm_dense(p, "d", x, 3);
  const Tensor v = p.at("d/v"), gg = p.at("d/g");
  const auto grads = nn::grad(nn::sum(wn.y * r), {v, gg, wn.W});
  nn::NoGradGuard ng;
  const Tensor nv = nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(v), {0})), nW = nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(wn.W), {0}));
  const Tensor dg = nn::sum(grads[2] * v, {0}) / nv;                 // (dL/dW_j) · v_j / ||v_j||
  const Tensor dv = gg / nv * (grads[2] - dg * v / nv);               // (g_j / ||v_j||)(dL/dW_j − (dL/dg_j) v_j / ||v_j||)
  const Tensor vdv = nn::sum(v * grads[0], {0});
  std::print("\nAfter setting g = (0.5, 1.5, 3):\n\n| column j | norm of v_j | g_j | norm of W_j | v_j · ∂L/∂v_j |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.1e} |\n", j, nv.to_vector<double>()[j], gg.to_vector<double>()[j], nW.to_vector<double>()[j], vdv.to_vector<double>()[j]);
  std::print("\nAutograd against the closed forms: max difference {:.1e} for ∂L/∂g and {:.1e} for ∂L/∂v.\n", fmax_abs(grads[1] - dg), fmax_abs(grads[0] - dv));

  // Spectral normalisation of a 64 × 32 Glorot-uniform matrix.
  nn::ParameterStore sp(2, F64);
  const Tensor W = sp.get("W", {64, 32}, nn::init::glorot_uniform());
  const auto full = nn::svd(W);
  const auto s = full.s.to_vector<double>();
  std::print("\nW is 64 × 32; SVD: σ1 = {:.6f}, σ2 = {:.6f}, σ32 = {:.6f}.\n\n| power iterations k | estimate | relative error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n", s[0], s[1], s[31]);
  const Tensor u0 = nn::normal({1, 32}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
  std::vector<double> ks, est;
  for (int k = 1; k <= 30; ++k) {
    const double sk = nn::spectral_norm_power_iteration(W, u0, k).sigma.item();
    ks.push_back(k), est.push_back(sk);
    if (k <= 3 || k == 5 || k == 10 || k == 20 || k == 30) std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.1e} |\n", k, sk, (s[0] - sk) / s[0]);
  }
  // One iteration per call with the vector stored between calls, as in training.
  nn::ParameterStore params(2, F64), state(3, F64);
  (void)params.get("d/W", {64, 32}, nn::init::glorot_uniform());
  std::print("\nOne iteration per call, u stored between calls (same W):");
  nn::ReparamDense sn;
  for (int step = 1; step <= 20; ++step) {
    sn = nn::spectral_norm_dense(params, state, "d", nn::zeros({1, 64}, F64), 32, 1);
    if (step == 1 || step == 2 || step == 5 || step == 10 || step == 20) std::print(" call {}: relative error {:.1e};", step, (s[0] - sn.sigma.item()) / s[0]);
  }
  std::print("\n");
  const double s_sn = nn::svd(sn.W, false).s.to_vector<double>()[0];
  // The gain ||x W_sn|| / ||x|| for 1000 Gaussian inputs, and along the top left singular vector.
  const Tensor xs = nn::normal({1000, 64}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
  const auto gains = (nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(nn::matmul(xs, sn.W)), {1})) / nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(xs), {1}))).to_vector<double>();
  const Tensor top = nn::reshape(nn::slice(full.u, {0, 0}, {64, 1}), {1, 64});
  const double gain_top = std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(nn::matmul(top, sn.W))).item());
  double fro2 = 0;
  for (double si : s) fro2 += si * si;
  double mean_gain = 0;
  for (double q : gains) mean_gain += q / gains.size();
  std::print("Largest singular value of W / σ̂ by SVD: {:.12f}.\nGain over 1000 random inputs: mean {:.4f}, range {:.4f} to {:.4f}; sqrt(Σσ_i² / 64) / σ1 = {:.4f}. Gain along the top left singular vector: {:.12f}.\n",
             s_sn, mean_gain, *std::ranges::min_element(gains), *std::ranges::max_element(gains), std::sqrt(fro2 / 64) / s[0], gain_top);

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.points(ks, est, "estimate", RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6).abline_h(s[0], RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5).abline_h(s[1], RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5);
    pl.text(4, s[0] + 0.025, "σ1 (SVD)", RGB{220, 38, 38}, 13).text(22, s[1] - 0.02, "σ2", RGB{100, 116, 139}, 13);
    pl.x_limits(0, 31).y_limits(1.4, 1.95).title("Power iteration for σ1").x_label("iterations k").y_label("estimate of σ1");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    std::vector<double> centres, counts;
    const double lo = 0.25, hi = 1.05, w = 0.0125;
    for (double c = lo + w / 2; c < hi; c += w) centres.push_back(c), counts.push_back(0);
    for (double q : gains) counts[std::min(counts.size() - 1, static_cast<std::size_t>(std::max(0.0, (q - lo) / w)))] += 1;
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.bars(centres, counts, "1000 Gaussian inputs", RGB{37, 99, 235}, w * 0.9).abline_v(1.0, RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2)
        .abline_v(std::sqrt(fro2 / 64) / s[0], RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.5);
    pl.text(0.86, 120, "bound 1 (along u1)", RGB{220, 38, 38}, 13).text(std::sqrt(fro2 / 64) / s[0] + 0.012, 120, "RMS prediction", RGB{17, 24, 39}, 13);
    pl.x_limits(lo, hi).y_limits(0, 170).title("Gain of the normalised layer").x_label("‖x W / σ̂‖ / ‖x‖").y_label("count");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/spectral_basics.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
