\image(figures/spectral_basics.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the power-iteration estimate of the largest singular value after k iterations (points), with σ1 from the SVD (red) and σ2 (grey). Right: histogram of the gain ‖xW/σ̂‖/‖x‖ of the normalised layer for 1000 Gaussian inputs, with the prediction from the Frobenius norm (black) and the bound 1 (red).)
\alttext(Left: the points rise from 1.45 at k = 1 through 1.66 and 1.73, cross the grey σ2 line between k = 3 and 4, and level off on the red σ1 line at 1.82 from about k = 12. Right: a bell-shaped histogram between 0.29 and 0.59 centred on the black line at 0.44; the red line at 1 is far to the right of all bars.)

At creation the effective weights equal \(v\) exactly. After the gains are set, every column of \(W\) has norm exactly \(g_j\), although the columns of \(v\) have norms 0.91, 1.46 and 0.67. Automatic differentiation agrees with the closed forms to \(4\times10^{-15}\), and \(v_j\cdot\partial L/\partial v_j\) vanishes to the same precision.

The matrix has \(\sigma_1=1.8234\) and \(\sigma_2=1.7405\), a ratio of 0.955, so power iteration is slow. One iteration underestimates by 21%, ten by 0.5% and thirty by \(5\times10^{-5}\). Storing the vector between calls gives about the same progress, one iteration per call, from a different random start: \(5.5\times10^{-3}\) after 10 calls and \(4.8\times10^{-4}\) after 20.
Because the estimate is still low, the normalised matrix has a spectral norm of 1.00048, slightly above 1, and the layer is 1-Lipschitz only in the limit. Random inputs are stretched by between 0.29 and 0.59, with mean 0.4376 against the Frobenius prediction 0.4392. Only the top left singular vector reaches the full gain, 1.00048.

> Checking the theory

>> Convergence, gradient and norm growth

The program runs power iteration for up to 40 iterations on three \(64\times32\) matrices: two built with \(\sigma_2/\sigma_1=0.5\) and 0.8, and the Glorot one from above. It compares every estimate with \(\sqrt{A_{4k}/A_{4k-2}}\) and the late error ratio per iteration with \((\sigma_2/\sigma_1)^4\). It differentiates the converged estimate and compares the result with \(U_1V_1^\top\).
It then trains a weight-normalised softmax regression on all 1797 digits by full-batch gradient descent, for 300 steps at three learning rates. At every step it checks the norm-growth identity and records the effective learning rate \(\eta g^2/\lVert v\rVert^2\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/spectral_theory.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  nn::Generator g(5);
  // Three 64 × 32 matrices: the Glorot-uniform one from before and two with a chosen gap, W = Q1 diag(s) Q2^T.
  nn::ParameterStore sp(2, F64);
  const Tensor Wg = sp.get("W", {64, 32}, nn::init::glorot_uniform());
  auto with_spectrum = [&](double ratio) {
    const Tensor Q1 = nn::qr(nn::normal({64, 32}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64)).q, Q2 = nn::qr(nn::normal({32, 32}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64)).q;
    std::vector<double> s(32);
    s[0] = 1.0;
    for (int i = 1; i < 32; ++i) s[i] = ratio * (1.0 - (i - 1) / 32.0);
    return nn::matmul(Q1 * nn::from_vector(s, {32}), Q2, false, true);
  };
  struct Case { std::string label; Tensor W; RGB color; };
  const std::vector<Case> cases{{"σ2/σ1 = 0.5", with_spectrum(0.5), {22, 163, 74}}, {"σ2/σ1 = 0.8", with_spectrum(0.8), {234, 88, 12}}, {"Glorot, σ2/σ1 = 0.955", Wg, {37, 99, 235}}};
  const Tensor u0 = nn::normal({1, 32}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::print("Power iteration against the exact formula sqrt(A_4k / A_(4k−2)), A_m = Σ c_i² σ_i^m, c = coefficients of u0 on the right singular vectors:\n\n");
  std::print("| matrix | σ2/σ1 | largest relative difference, k = 1..40 | error at k = 10 | measured error ratio per iteration (window) | (σ2/σ1)^4 |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& c : cases) {
    const auto d = nn::svd(c.W);
    const auto s = d.s.to_vector<double>();
    const auto coef = nn::matmul(u0, d.v).to_vector<double>();
    auto A = [&](int m) { double a = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 32; ++i) a += coef[i] * coef[i] * std::pow(s[i] / s[0], m); return a; };
    std::vector<double> ks, err, kf, errf;
    double worst = 0, e10 = 0;
    std::vector<double> errs;
    for (int k = 1; k <= 40; ++k) {
      const double est = nn::spectral_norm_power_iteration(c.W, u0, k).sigma.item();
      const double exact = s[0] * std::sqrt(A(4 * k) / A(4 * k - 2));
      worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(est - exact) / exact);
      const double e = (s[0] - est) / s[0], ef = (s[0] - exact) / s[0];
      if (k == 10) e10 = e;
      errs.push_back(e);
      if (e > 1e-15) ks.push_back(k), err.push_back(std::log10(e));
      if (ef > 1e-15) kf.push_back(k), errf.push_back(std::log10(ef));
    }
    // Rate over the last 5 iterations whose errors are well above the 1e-12 floor set by the normalisation guard.
    int last = 0;
    for (int k = 1; k <= 40; ++k) if (errs[k - 1] > 1e-10) last = k;
    const int first = std::max(1, last - 5);
    std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} | {:.3f} (k = {} to {}) | {:.3f} |\n", c.label, s[1] / s[0], worst, e10, std::pow(errs[last - 1] / errs[first - 1], 1.0 / (last - first)), first, last,
               std::pow(s[1] / s[0], 4));
    pl.points(ks, err, c.label, c.color, 5).lines(kf, errf, c.color, 1.5);
  }
  pl.x_limits(0, 41).y_limits(-16.5, 4).title("Power-iteration error").x_label("iterations k").y_label("log10 relative error");
  fig.add(pl);

  // The gradient of the converged estimate is the outer product of the top singular vectors, u1 v1^T in W = U S V^T.
  {
    Tensor W = Wg.detach().clone();
    W.requires_grad_();
    const auto e = nn::spectral_norm_power_iteration(W, u0, 400);
    const Tensor gW = nn::grad(e.sigma, {W})[0];
    const auto d = nn::svd(Wg);
    const Tensor outer = nn::matmul(nn::slice(d.u, {0, 0}, {64, 1}), nn::slice(d.v, {0, 0}, {32, 1}), false, true);
    std::print("\nGradient of σ̂ after 400 iterations against u1 v1^T from the SVD: max difference {:.1e} (sign fixed by σ̂ > 0: {:.1e}).\n",
               std::min(nn::max(nn::abs(gW - outer)).item(), nn::max(nn::abs(gW + outer)).item()), nn::max(nn::abs(nn::abs(gW) - nn::abs(outer))).item());
  }

  // Weight normalisation under plain gradient descent: since ∂L/∂v_j ⊥ v_j, ||v_j||² grows by exactly η² ||∂L/∂v_j||² per step.
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797;
  std::vector<double> px(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * 64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto col = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) px[i * 64 + j] = col[i] / 16.0;
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(px, {n, 64});
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  auto pr = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::print("\nWeight-normalised softmax regression on all 1797 digits, full-batch gradient descent, 300 steps:\n\n| learning rate η | Σ_j norm(v_j)² at start | at end | largest per-step identity error | effective learning rate η g²/norm(v)², start → end |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const std::vector<std::pair<double, RGB>> rates{{0.5, {22, 163, 74}}, {2.0, {234, 88, 12}}, {8.0, {220, 38, 38}}};
  for (const auto& [eta, color] : rates) {
    nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
    (void)nn::weight_norm_dense(p, "d", nn::slice(X, {0, 0}, {1, 64}), 10);
    std::vector<double> steps, norm2, logn;
    double worst = 0, first_eff = 0, last_eff = 0;
    for (int t = 0; t <= 300; ++t) {
      const Tensor v = p.at("d/v"), gg = p.at("d/g"), b = p.at("d/b");
      const double before = nn::sum(nn::square(v)).item();
      // Effective learning rate for the part of each column orthogonal to it, η g_j² / ||v_j||², averaged over the 10 columns.
      const double eff = eta * nn::mean(nn::square(gg) / nn::sum(nn::square(v), {0})).item();
      if (t == 0) first_eff = eff;
      last_eff = eff;
      steps.push_back(t), norm2.push_back(before);
      logn.push_back(std::log10(before));
      if (t == 300) break;
      const auto grads = nn::grad(nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(nn::weight_norm_dense(p, "d", X, 10).y, y), {v, gg, b});
      nn::NoGradGuard ng;
      const Tensor vn = v - eta * grads[0];
      const Tensor change = nn::sum(nn::square(vn), {0}) - nn::sum(nn::square(v), {0}) - eta * eta * nn::sum(nn::square(grads[0]), {0});
      worst = std::max(worst, nn::max(nn::abs(change)).item() / before);
      p.set("d/v", vn), p.set("d/g", gg - eta * grads[1]), p.set("d/b", b - eta * grads[2]);
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.1e} | {:.3f} → {:.3f} |\n", eta, norm2.front(), norm2.back(), worst, first_eff, last_eff);
    pr.lines(steps, logn, std::format("η = {}", eta), color, 2);
  }
  pr.x_limits(0, 310).y_limits(1, 4.5).title("Weight norm: growth of the directions").x_label("gradient-descent step").y_label("log10 Σ_j ‖v_j‖²");
  fig.add(pr);
  fig.size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/spectral_theory.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Power iteration against the exact formula sqrt(A_4k / A_(4k−2)), A_m = Σ c_i² σ_i^m, c = coefficients of u0 on the right singular vectors:

| matrix | σ2/σ1 | largest relative difference, k = 1..40 | error at k = 10 | measured error ratio per iteration (window) | (σ2/σ1)^4 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| σ2/σ1 = 0.5 | 0.500 | 6.1e-12 | 3.4e-11 | 0.058 (k = 4 to 9) | 0.062 |
| σ2/σ1 = 0.8 | 0.800 | 3.6e-12 | 1.9e-04 | 0.361 (k = 19 to 24) | 0.410 |
| Glorot, σ2/σ1 = 0.955 | 0.955 | 7.3e-13 | 1.9e-02 | 0.825 (k = 35 to 40) | 0.830 |

Gradient of σ̂ after 400 iterations against u1 v1^T from the SVD: max difference 3.4e-14 (sign fixed by σ̂ > 0: 3.4e-14).

Weight-normalised softmax regression on all 1797 digits, full-batch gradient descent, 300 steps:

| learning rate η | Σ_j norm(v_j)² at start | at end | largest per-step identity error | effective learning rate η g²/norm(v)², start → end |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.5 | 16.8336 | 18.3008 | 1.3e-16 | 0.500 → 4.439 |
| 2 | 16.8336 | 104.2946 | 1.5e-16 | 2.000 → 7.464 |
| 8 | 16.8336 | 1747.2744 | 8.3e-16 | 8.000 → 3.584 |
\image(figures/spectral_theory.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: relative error of the power-iteration estimate (points, log scale) against the exact formula (lines) for three spectral gaps. Right: total squared norm of the directions during weight-normalised gradient descent on the digits (log scale), for three learning rates.)
\alttext(Left: three straight descending lines of points: green (gap 0.5) falls fastest and flattens at 10 to the minus 12 from k = 12, orange (0.8) reaches the same floor at k = 29, and blue (0.955) falls slowly to 10 to the minus 5 at k = 40. The lines follow the points until each reaches the floor and then continue below it. Right: the green curve (eta = 0.5) stays near 10 to the 1.26; the orange (eta = 2) rises to 10 to the 1.8 by step 100 and jumps to 10 to the 2 near step 265; the red (eta = 8) shoots up to 10 to the 3.24 within 20 steps and stays flat.)

The exact formula predicts every estimate to within \(6.1\times10^{-12}\), \(3.6\times10^{-12}\) and \(7.3\times10^{-13}\). The floor at \(10^{-12}\) in the figure is not rounding. It is the bias introduced by the \(10^{-12}\) added to squared norms in the normalisation, which keeps the estimate defined for a zero vector.
Before the floor, the measured error ratio per iteration is 0.058 for the 0.5 gap (\(0.5^4=0.062\)) and 0.825 for the Glorot matrix (\(0.955^4=0.830\)). For the 0.8 gap it is 0.361 against 0.410: the next singular values (0.775, 0.75, …) still contribute terms that decay a little faster, so the ratio is still approaching its limit from below. The gradient of the converged estimate equals \(U_1V_1^\top\) to \(3.4\times10^{-14}\).

The norm-growth identity holds at every step to a relative error of at most \(8.3\times10^{-16}\). With \(\eta=0.5\) the directions barely grow (16.8 to 18.3). With \(\eta=2\) they grow to 104, and with \(\eta=8\) to 1747, almost all of it in the first 20 steps. The effective learning rates end at 4.4, 7.5 and 3.6.
The smallest step's effective rate grew because its gains grew, which is how a softmax regression sharpens its predictions. The largest step's effective rate fell because its directions grew. Starting rates that differ by a factor of 16 end within a factor of about 2. This is the stabilisation Salimans and Kingma describe. The norm growth behind it follows the identity exactly; how far the effective rates converge depends on the problem.

> A dataset: certified robustness on digits

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) has 1797 handwritten digits of \(8\times8\) pixels, scaled to \([0,1]\). The first 1437 train and the last 360 test. Four MLPs with two hidden ReLU layers of 128 units are trained with Adam (step \(10^{-3}\)) for 3000 batches of 64:
- a plain network;
- one with weight normalisation in every layer;
- two with spectral normalisation in every layer (one power iteration per step).

A spectrally normalised network multiplies its logits by a fixed scale \(c=5\) or 20, so that its nominal Lipschitz constant is \(c\). Without that scale, two logits could differ by at most \(\sqrt2\) times the distance between inputs, which keeps the loss high.
For each network the program computes the exact Lipschitz bound of the logits and the certified radius of every test image. It also attacks every test image with 20 steps of \(\ell_2\) PGD at budgets from 0.1 to 1. For scale, a test image has Euclidean norm 3.9 on average.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/spectral_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F32 = nn::DType::Float32;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, n_train = 1437, n_test = n - n_train;
  std::vector<float> px(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * 64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto col = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) px[i * 64 + j] = static_cast<float>(col[i] / 16.0);
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(px, {n, 64});
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::slice(X, {0, 0}, {n_train, 64}), ytr = nn::slice(y, {0}, {n_train});
  const Tensor Xte = nn::slice(X, {n_train, 0}, {n_test, 64}), yte = nn::slice(y, {n_train}, {n_test});
  std::print("Test images: mean Euclidean norm {:.2f} (pixels in [0, 1], 64 per image).\n\n", nn::mean(nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(Xte), {1}))).item());

  // MLP 64-128-128-10 with ReLU, with plain, weight-normalised or spectrally normalised layers; the spectrally
  // normalised network multiplies its logits by a fixed scale c, which is then its Lipschitz constant.
  enum Kind { Plain, WeightNorm, Spectral };
  struct Model { Kind kind; double scale; std::string label; RGB color; };
  const std::vector<Model> models{{Plain, 1, "plain", {17, 24, 39}}, {WeightNorm, 1, "weight norm", {22, 163, 74}},
                                  {Spectral, 5, "spectral norm, c = 5", {37, 99, 235}}, {Spectral, 20, "spectral norm, c = 20", {234, 88, 12}}};
  const std::vector<double> eps{0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0};
  const int steps = 3000;
  std::print("| model | test accuracy | Lipschitz bound of the logits | median certified radius | certified / PGD accuracy at ε = 0.1 | 0.25 | 0.5 | 0.75 | 1 |\n");
  std::print("| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> losses(models.size()), certified(models.size()), empirical(models.size());
  std::string sigma_note;
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < models.size(); ++m) {
    const auto& M = models[m];
    nn::ParameterStore p(1, F32), state(2, F32);
    std::vector<Tensor> Ws;
    auto f = [&](const Tensor& x, bool train) {
      Ws.clear();
      Tensor h = x;
      const std::vector<std::int64_t> widths{128, 128, 10};
      for (std::size_t l = 0; l < widths.size(); ++l) {
        const auto act = l + 1 < widths.size() ? nn::Activation::Relu : nn::Activation::None;
        const std::string name = std::format("l{}", l);
        if (M.kind == Plain) {
          h = nn::dense(p, name, h, widths[l], {.activation = act});
          Ws.push_back(p.at(name + "/W"));
        } else if (M.kind == WeightNorm) {
          const auto r = nn::weight_norm_dense(p, name, h, widths[l], act);
          h = r.y, Ws.push_back(r.W);
        } else {
          const auto r = nn::spectral_norm_dense(p, state, name, h, widths[l], 1, act, train);
          h = r.y, Ws.push_back(r.W);
        }
      }
      return M.kind == Spectral ? h * M.scale : h;
    };
    { nn::NoGradGuard ng; (void)f(nn::slice(Xtr, {0, 0}, {2, 64}), false); }
    nn::Optimizer opt(p, 1e-3, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
    std::mt19937_64 rng(1);
    std::vector<std::int64_t> order(n_train);
    std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
    std::size_t pos = n_train;
    double running = 0;
    for (int s = 1; s <= steps; ++s) {
      if (pos + 64 > order.size()) std::shuffle(order.begin(), order.end(), rng), pos = 0;
      const Tensor idx = nn::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>(order.begin() + pos, order.begin() + pos + 64), {64});
      pos += 64;
      const Tensor loss = nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(nn::gather(Xtr, idx, 0), true), nn::gather(ytr, idx, 0));
      running += loss.item() / 50;
      opt.step(nn::grad(loss, p.parameters()));
      if (s % 50 == 0) losses[m].push_back(running), running = 0;
    }
    // Lipschitz bound of the logits: product of the exact spectral norms of the effective weights, times c.
    Tensor logits;
    { nn::NoGradGuard ng; logits = f(Xte, false); }
    const double L = nn::lipschitz_bound(Ws) * (M.kind == Spectral ? M.scale : 1.0);
    if (M.kind == Spectral) {
      std::string s = std::format("{}, after training, per layer: stored estimate / exact σ1, and σ2/σ1:", M.label);
      for (std::size_t l = 0; l < 3; ++l) {
        const Tensor W = p.at(std::format("l{}/W", l));
        const double est = nn::spectral_norm_power_iteration(W, state.at(std::format("l{}/u", l)), 1).sigma.item();
        const auto sv = nn::svd(nn::cast(W, nn::DType::Float64), false).s.to_vector<double>();
        s += std::format(" layer {}: {:.4f}, {:.4f};", l + 1, est / sv[0], sv[1] / sv[0]);
      }
      sigma_note += s + "\n";
    }
    // Certified radius (Tsuzuku et al. 2018): if the margin between the true logit and the largest other one is μ, no ℓ2
    // perturbation smaller than μ / (√2 L) can change the prediction.
    const auto lv = logits.to_vector<float>();
    const auto yv = yte.to_vector<std::int64_t>();
    std::vector<double> radius(n_test);
    int correct = 0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n_test; ++i) {
      const float* row = &lv[i * 10];
      float other = -1e30f;
      for (int k = 0; k < 10; ++k) if (k != yv[i]) other = std::max(other, row[k]);
      const double margin = row[yv[i]] - other;
      correct += margin > 0;
      radius[i] = margin > 0 ? margin / (std::sqrt(2.0) * L) : 0.0;
    }
    auto rs = radius;
    std::ranges::sort(rs);
    for (double e : eps) {
      int c = 0;
      for (double r : radius) c += r >= e;
      certified[m].push_back(static_cast<double>(c) / n_test);
    }
    // PGD in the ℓ2 ball (Madry et al. 2018): 20 normalised-gradient steps of size 2.5 ε / 20, projected onto the ball and [0, 1].
    for (double e : eps) {
      Tensor delta = nn::zeros({n_test, 64}, F32);
      for (int it = 0; it < 20; ++it) {
        Tensor d = delta.detach().clone();
        d.requires_grad_();
        const Tensor gd = nn::grad(nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(Xte + d, false), yte), {d})[0];
        nn::NoGradGuard ng;
        const Tensor step = gd / (nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(gd), {1}, true)) + 1e-12);
        Tensor nd = d + 2.5 * e / 20 * step;
        nd = nd / nn::maximum(nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(nd), {1}, true)) / e, nn::scalar(1.0f));
        delta = nn::clip(Xte + nd, 0.0, 1.0) - Xte;
      }
      nn::NoGradGuard ng;
      empirical[m].push_back(nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(f(Xte + delta, false), -1), yte), F32)).item());
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.1f}% | {:.1f} | {:.3f} |", M.label, 100.0 * correct / n_test, L, rs[n_test / 2]);
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < eps.size(); ++k) std::print(" {:.1f}% / {:.1f}% |", 100 * certified[m][k], 100 * empirical[m][k]);
    std::print("\n");
  }
  std::print("\n{}", sigma_note);

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::size_t m = 0; m < models.size(); ++m) {
      std::vector<double> xs, ys;
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < losses[m].size(); ++k) xs.push_back(50.0 * (k + 1)), ys.push_back(std::log10(losses[m][k]));
      pl.lines(xs, ys, models[m].label, models[m].color, 2);
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, steps * 1.02).y_limits(-4, 1.6).title("Training loss").x_label("training step").y_label("log10 cross-entropy (mean of 50 batches)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::size_t m = 0; m < models.size(); ++m) {
      std::vector<double> ys;
      for (double a : empirical[m]) ys.push_back(100 * a);
      pl.lines(eps, ys, models[m].label, models[m].color, 2).points(eps, ys, models[m].color, 6);
      std::vector<double> cy;
      for (double a : certified[m]) cy.push_back(100 * a);
      pl.lines(eps, cy, models[m].color, 1).points(eps, cy, models[m].color, 9);
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, 1.05).y_limits(0, 150).y_ticks({0, 20, 40, 60, 80, 100}, {"0", "20", "40", "60", "80", "100"})
        .title("Robust accuracy").x_label("ℓ2 perturbation budget ε").y_label("test accuracy (%)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 500).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/spectral_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Test images: mean Euclidean norm 3.88 (pixels in [0, 1], 64 per image).

| model | test accuracy | Lipschitz bound of the logits | median certified radius | certified / PGD accuracy at ε = 0.1 | 0.25 | 0.5 | 0.75 | 1 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| plain | 91.9% | 64.7 | 0.138 | 68.3% / 87.2% | 1.9% / 73.9% | 0.0% / 42.5% | 0.0% / 13.3% | 0.0% / 0.8% |
| weight norm | 92.5% | 46.1 | 0.176 | 75.3% / 88.9% | 17.5% / 77.5% | 0.0% / 48.9% | 0.0% / 18.6% | 0.0% / 2.2% |
| spectral norm, c = 5 | 89.2% | 6.3 | 0.383 | 83.9% / 86.4% | 68.1% / 80.8% | 27.8% / 63.9% | 0.3% / 40.3% | 0.0% / 13.6% |
| spectral norm, c = 20 | 93.1% | 22.2 | 0.314 | 86.1% / 89.7% | 63.3% / 80.6% | 7.8% / 53.1% | 0.0% / 17.8% | 0.0% / 1.4% |

spectral norm, c = 5, after training, per layer: stored estimate / exact σ1, and σ2/σ1: layer 1: 0.9388, 0.9529; layer 2: 0.8821, 0.9379; layer 3: 0.9612, 0.9571;
spectral norm, c = 20, after training, per layer: stored estimate / exact σ1, and σ2/σ1: layer 1: 0.9690, 0.9856; layer 2: 0.9509, 0.9805; layer 3: 0.9768, 0.9847;
\image(figures/spectral_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: training loss (mean over 50 batches, log scale). Right: test accuracy under attack. Lines with small points are the accuracy under PGD, an upper bound on robust accuracy; thin lines with large points are the certified accuracy, a lower bound.)
\alttext(Left: the plain, weight-norm and c = 20 curves fall to between 10 to the minus 3 and 10 to the minus 3.4 by step 3000, while the c = 5 curve flattens near 10 to the minus 0.75. Right: at budget 0.1 all models are between 68 and 90%. With growing budget the PGD lines fall; the blue c = 5 line stays highest, at 64% at 0.5 and 14% at 1. The large certified points fall faster: blue keeps 68% at 0.25 and 28% at 0.5, orange 63% and 8%, green 18% and 0, black 2% and 0.)

The plain network reaches 91.9% test accuracy, weight normalisation 92.5%, and spectral normalisation 89.2% with \(c=5\) and 93.1% with \(c=20\). Training with \(c=5\) stalls at a loss near 0.18, plausibly because a network with a Lipschitz bound of 6 cannot separate the logits of nearby images of different classes by much.
The Lipschitz bound of the plain network is 64.7, so its median certified radius is only 0.138, and it certifies 1.9% of the test images at 0.25. Weight normalisation happens to end with a lower bound, 46.1, and certifies 17.5%. Spectral normalisation certifies 68.1% (\(c=5\)) and 63.3% (\(c=20\)) at 0.25, and 27.8% and 7.8% at 0.5. Under PGD at 0.5, the plain network keeps 42.5% and the \(c=5\) network 63.9%. Every certified accuracy lies below the corresponding PGD accuracy, as it must.

The bound of the \(c=5\) network is 6.3, not 5. After training, its stored power-iteration estimates are 0.939, 0.882 and 0.961 of the true spectral norms, and \(5/(0.939\cdot0.882\cdot0.961)=6.28\). The trained layers have \(\sigma_2/\sigma_1\) between 0.94 and 0.99. With fixed weights, 3000 iterations at those gaps would have converged completely. The lag must come from the weights moving faster than one iteration per step can track. The certified radii use the exact bound, so they remain valid, but the nominal constant \(c\) is not the real one.

> Conclusion

Weight normalisation does what its algebra says: column norms equal the gains, the direction's gradient is orthogonal to the direction, and the squared norm of the direction grows by exactly \(\eta^2\lVert\nabla\rVert^2\) per step. That growth lowers the effective learning rate of large steps, while the gains can raise it for small ones. On the digits, learning rates 16 times apart ended within a factor of about 2.

Power iteration underestimates the spectral norm and converges at the rate \((\sigma_2/\sigma_1)^4\) per iteration, exactly as the formula in the singular values says. That rate is slow for realistic weight matrices, whose top singular values are close together. Spectral normalisation gave digit classifiers a far smaller Lipschitz bound than plain training and certified most test images against perturbations of a quarter of a pixel's full range in Euclidean norm. With one iteration per step, the true constant was 26% above the nominal one. A guarantee should therefore use exact singular values, as `nn::lipschitz_bound` does, not the training-time estimate.

> References

- T. Salimans and D. P. Kingma (2016). "Weight normalization: a simple reparameterization to accelerate training of deep neural networks". *Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems* 29.
- T. Miyato, T. Kataoka, M. Koyama and Y. Yoshida (2018). "Spectral normalization for generative adversarial networks". *ICLR*.
- Y. Tsuzuku, I. Sato and M. Sugiyama (2018). "Lipschitz-margin training: scalable certification of perturbation invariance for deep neural networks". *Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems* 31.
- A. Madry, A. Makelov, L. Schmidt, D. Tsipras and A. Vladu (2018). "Towards deep learning models resistant to adversarial attacks". *ICLR*.
- G. H. Golub and C. F. Van Loan (2013). *Matrix Computations*, 4th edition. Johns Hopkins University Press.
- E. Alpaydin and C. Kaynak (1998). "Optical recognition of handwritten digits". UCI Machine Learning Repository.
